      "message": "Note to self: verify the following automated claims\n\nHere is a detailed mapping of how the new code in  CFX_AndroidFontInfo::IsBetterMatch  reproduces the logic of the old         \n  CFPF_SkiaFontMgr::CreateFont .                                                                                                 \n                                                                                                                                 \n  The key change in the new architecture ( CFX_FolderFontInfo ) is that name matching is separated from similarity scoring.      \n                                                                                                                                 \n  • In the old code, name matching was a huge weight (+1000 for exact, +500 for substitution) added to a single  score .         \n  • In the new code, name matching is handled by the  must_match_name  flag and explicit checks, while  candidate_score  only    \n  represents style similarity (weight, italic, serif, etc.).                                                                     \n  ──────                                                                                                                         \n  ### Case 1: Non-CJK Fonts                                                                                                      \n                                                                                                                                 \n  In  IsBetterMatch , we immediately delegate to the base class for non-CJK:                                                     \n                                                                                                                                 \n      if (!FX_CharSetIsCJK(charset)) {                                                                                           \n        return CFX_FolderFontInfo::IsBetterMatch(candidate, candidate_score,                                                     \n                                                 current_best, current_best_score,                                               \n                                                 charset, family, must_match_name);                                              \n      }                                                                                                                          \n                                                                                                                                 \n  How it maps to the old behavior:                                                                                               \n                                                                                                                                 \n  • Old Code: The total score was  name_weight + style_score . Because  name_weight  (1000 or 500) was much larger than the      \n  maximum possible  style_score  (~224), any font that matched the name (or substitution) would always beat any font that did not,\n  regardless of style.                                                                                                           \n  • New Code ( CFX_FolderFontInfo::IsBetterMatch ):\n      • If  must_match_name  is true, it rejects any candidate that doesn\u0027t match the name. Among those that match, it returns   \n      true  if  candidate_score \u003e current_best_score .\n      • This achieves the exact same result: only matching fonts are considered, and the one with the best style match wins.     \n\n  ──────\n  ### Case 2: CJK Fonts with  must_match_name \u003d true \n\n  This occurs when we are looking for a specific CJK substitution (e.g., trying to find \"Droid Sans Fallback\").\n\n      if (must_match_name) {\n        ByteStringView family_view \u003d family.AsStringView();\n        if (candidate-\u003eface_name_ !\u003d family \u0026\u0026\n            !FindFamilyNameMatch(family_view, candidate-\u003eface_name_)) {\n          return false; // Reject if candidate doesn\u0027t match the requested name.\n        }\n        if (current_best) {\n          if (candidate_score !\u003d current_best_score) {\n            return candidate_score \u003e current_best_score; // Prefer better style.\n          }\n          return candidate-\u003eglyph_count_ \u003e current_best-\u003eglyph_count_; // Tie-breaker: more glyphs.\n        }\n        return true; // First matching font becomes current_best.\n      }\n\n  How it maps to the old behavior:\n\n  • Old Code: For CJK, if  matches_name  was true, the font was selected:\n    if (matches_name || font-\u003eglyph_num \u003e best_glyph_num) { ... }\n  If multiple fonts matched the name (e.g., \"Droid Sans Fallback Regular\" and \"Droid Sans Fallback Bold\"), the old code had an   \n  iteration-order bug: the last one scanned would overwrite the previous one because  matches_name  was true for both, regardless\n  of which one was a better style match.\n  • New Code: We fix this order-dependency. We still require the name to match (line 219), but if we have multiple matches, we   \n  prefer the one that matches the requested style ( candidate_score \u003e current_best_score ). If the styles match equally well, we \n  take the one with more glyphs.\n  ──────\n  ### Case 3: CJK Fonts with  must_match_name \u003d false \n\n  This is the final fallback for CJK. We failed to find the font by name or substitution, and we just need any CJK font that can \n  display the characters.\n\n      uint32_t current_best_glyphs \u003d current_best ? current_best-\u003eglyph_count_ : 0;\n      if (candidate-\u003eglyph_count_ !\u003d current_best_glyphs) {\n        return candidate-\u003eglyph_count_ \u003e current_best_glyphs; // Primary: prefer font with more glyphs.\n      }\n      return candidate_score \u003e current_best_score; // Tie-breaker: prefer better style match.\n\n  How it maps to the old behavior:\n\n  • Old Code: In CJK fallback, we wanted the font with the most glyphs to avoid missing characters (tofu):\n    if (matches_name || font-\u003eglyph_num \u003e best_glyph_num) {\n      best_font \u003d font.get();\n      best_glyph_num \u003d font-\u003eglyph_num;\n    }\n  If no fonts matched the name, the condition simplified to  font-\u003eglyph_num \u003e best_glyph_num , which scanned for the maximum    \n  glyph count.\n  • New Code: We explicitly compare glyph counts first (line 230). The font with the highest glyph count wins. If two fonts have \n  the same glyph count, we use the style similarity score as a tie-breaker (line 233). This is cleaner and guarantees we get the \n  largest font.",
